ter a bed bath"}]]}],["$","tr","4",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"After touching patient surroundings"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"After changing linens or adjusting a bed rail"}]]}]]}]]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"text-sm text-gray-500 mt-2 italic","children":"Hand hygiene moments in direct care"}]]}]
19:["$","div","3",{"className":"p-4 rounded-xl border-l-4 text-gray-800 leading-relaxed bg-yellow-50 border-yellow-400","children":[false,"Gloves are not a substitute for hand hygiene, and the same gloves must never move between patients or between a dirty and a clean task on the same patient. Changing gloves after perineal care and before touching a wound — with hand hygiene between — is not excessive caution; it is the difference between care and cross-contamination."]}]
